  <section class="section" id="s1">
    <h2>1. What is Metasploit?</h2>
    <p>Imagine you are a licensed locksmith hired by a company to test the physical security of their building. Your job is to find every door, window, and skylight that could be opened by an intruder — and report them before a real criminal does. <strong>Metasploit is exactly that, but for computer systems.</strong></p>
    <p>Metasploit is an open-source penetration testing framework developed by H.D. Moore in 2003 and later acquired by Rapid7 in 2009. It is the world's most widely used exploitation framework, used by security researchers, ethical hackers, and penetration testers to discover, validate, and demonstrate security vulnerabilities in systems.</p>

    <h3>A Brief History</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Milestone</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2003</td><td>H.D. Moore creates Metasploit in Perl with 11 exploits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2005</td><td>Rewritten in Ruby; gains community momentum</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2007</td><td>Metasploit 3.0 — major modular architecture overhaul</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2009</td><td>Rapid7 acquires Metasploit; commercial editions launched</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2011</td><td>Metasploit 4.0 — 900+ modules, massive expansion</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2024</td><td>3,000+ modules; industry-standard tool for red teams globally</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Why Does Metasploit Matter?</h3>
    <p>Every software has bugs. Some of those bugs are <strong>security vulnerabilities</strong> — weaknesses that attackers can abuse to gain unauthorized access, steal data, or disrupt operations. Metasploit packages known vulnerabilities into ready-to-use modules, letting security professionals test whether a system is vulnerable <em>before</em> a real attacker finds it.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">⚡ The Core Idea</div>
      <p>Metasploit does not create vulnerabilities. It <em>reveals</em> and <em>demonstrates</em> ones that already exist in unpatched or misconfigured systems. The goal is always to fix them first.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Editions of Metasploit</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Metasploit Framework (free)</strong> — Command-line tool; fully open-source; used in this guide.</li>
      <li><strong>Metasploit Pro (commercial)</strong> — GUI, automated scanning, team collaboration, reporting.</li>
      <li><strong>Metasploit Express</strong> — Simplified scanning and exploitation for smaller teams.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For learning purposes, the <strong>free Framework edition</strong> is everything you need. It comes pre-installed in Kali Linux and Parrot OS.</p>
  </section>

