    <h2>3. Payloads — Your Mission Cargo</h2>
    <p>If an exploit is the key that unlocks a door, a <strong>payload</strong> is what you carry through that door. It is the actual code that runs on the target system once the exploit succeeds. Choosing the right payload is critical — it determines what you can do after gaining access.</p>

    <h3>Three Families of Payloads</h3>

    <div class="step-flow">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Singles (Inline Payloads)</h4>
          <p>Self-contained, all-in-one payloads. They do one job and exit. Think of them as a note slipped under a door — it says everything it needs to in one go. Example: <span class="mono">windows/exec</span> — just runs a command and exits. Small, reliable, but limited.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Stagers + Stages (Two-Part Payloads)</h4>
          <p>A <strong>stager</strong> is a tiny piece of code that opens a channel back to you. Then it downloads the real, feature-rich <strong>stage</strong> payload. This two-step approach is used when you have very limited space in the exploit — like fitting through a narrow window to then open the front door from inside. Example: <span class="mono">windows/meterpreter/reverse_tcp</span> — stager creates TCP connection; stage delivers the full Meterpreter shell.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Stagerless (Singles with Stage Logic)</h4>
          <p>A single payload that contains everything but is still delivered in stages internally. Denoted by an underscore: <span class="mono">windows/meterpreter_reverse_tcp</span> (vs. <span class="mono">reverse_tcp</span> with a slash). Larger but does not require a second download, making it more reliable in restricted networks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Connection Directions: Bind vs. Reverse</h3>
    <p>This is one of the most important concepts beginners miss. Payloads communicate either by <em>receiving</em> an incoming connection or by <em>initiating</em> an outgoing one:</p>

