  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section class="section" id="s4">
    <h2>4. Exploits — The Lock Picks</h2>
    <p>An <strong>exploit</strong> is code that takes advantage of a specific bug in software or hardware. The name comes from "exploiting" a weakness. Bugs become exploits when a researcher discovers that a flaw can be used to make a program behave in an unintended way — often executing attacker-supplied code.</p>

    <h3>How Vulnerabilities Become Exploits</h3>
    <div class="step-flow">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Vulnerability Discovered</h4>
          <p>A researcher, bug bounty hunter, or attacker finds a flaw in software. It could be a buffer overflow, SQL injection, type confusion, or uninitialized memory.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>CVE Assigned</h4>
          <p>The vulnerability receives a <strong>CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures)</strong> identifier — e.g., CVE-2017-0144. This is like a case number at a police station. It allows the global security community to reference the same flaw consistently.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Proof-of-Concept Code Written</h4>
          <p>A developer writes code that demonstrates the vulnerability is real and exploitable. This PoC is often shared for research purposes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Metasploit Module Created</h4>
          <p>A community contributor or Rapid7 packages the exploit into a Metasploit module with configurable options, making it easy for pentesters to test systems at scale.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Patch Released → Pentesters Verify Patch</h4>
          <p>Vendors release patches. Pentesters then use the Metasploit module to confirm that patched systems are no longer vulnerable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Exploit Ranking System</h3>
    <p>Metasploit rates each exploit module by reliability. This helps you choose the right exploit for your situation:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Excellent</td><td>Will not crash the service; very reliable; safe for production testing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Great</td><td>Works reliably but may have minor instability</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Good</td><td>Reliable under normal conditions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Normal</td><td>Reliable if conditions are met (version, config)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Average</td><td>Sometimes works; may need manual tuning</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Low</td><td>Unreliable; may crash the target</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Manual</td><td>Requires significant manual input; not automated</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout success">
      <div class="callout-title">✅ Best Practice</div>
      <p>In real engagements, always prefer <strong>Excellent</strong> or <strong>Great</strong> ranked exploits. Lower-ranked modules can crash production services, causing unplanned downtime — a very bad outcome when you are supposed to be helping the client.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Famous Exploits in Metasploit</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>EternalBlue (ms17_010)</strong> — The NSA-developed exploit leaked by Shadow Brokers in 2017 that powers WannaCry ransomware. Targets unpatched Windows SMB.</li>
      <li><strong>MS08-067</strong> — Windows Server Service vulnerability; worked on most unpatched Windows XP/2003. Still present in Metasploitable 2.</li>
      <li><strong>Shellshock (CVE-2014-6271)</strong> — Bash vulnerability that allowed command injection through environment variables; affected millions of web servers.</li>
      <li><strong>BlueKeep (CVE-2019-0708)</strong> — Critical RDP vulnerability in Windows 7/Server 2008 — wormable, no authentication required.</li>
      <li><strong>Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228)</strong> — Remote code execution via Log4j logging library; affected hundreds of thousands of apps worldwide in 2021.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section class="section" id="s5">
    <h2>5. Auxiliary Modules — The Scout Team</h2>
    <p>Before a military unit attacks an objective, scouts go in first — they observe, map, and report back. In Metasploit, <strong>auxiliary modules</strong> are your scouts. They do not exploit systems; they <em>gather information</em> about them so you know where and how to attack.</p>

    <h3>Categories of Auxiliary Modules</h3>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Sub-path</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Scanners</td><td>auxiliary/scanner/</td><td>Port scanning, service detection, version fingerprinting</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fuzzers</td><td>auxiliary/fuzzer/</td><td>Send malformed data to find crash bugs</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Brute Forcers</td><td>auxiliary/scanner/*/login</td><td>Credential stuffing and password guessing</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sniffers</td><td>auxiliary/sniffer/</td><td>Capture network traffic passively</td></tr>
          <tr><td>DoS</td><td>auxiliary/dos/</td><td>Denial-of-service testing (use only with permission!)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>OSINT / Info</td><td>auxiliary/gather/</td><td>Passive intelligence gathering from public sources</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Key Scanner Modules You Should Know</h3>

    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-label">MSF Console — Useful Auxiliary Examples</div>
      <pre><span class="cmt"># TCP Port Scanner — fast ping sweep</span>
<span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">auxiliary/scanner/portscan/tcp</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span> <span class="val">192.168.1.0/24</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">PORTS</span>  <span class="val">1-1024</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>

<span class="cmt"># SMB Version Fingerprint</span>
<span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">auxiliary/scanner/smb/smb_version</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span> <span class="val">192.168.1.10</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>

<span class="cmt"># FTP Anonymous Login Check</span>
<span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">auxiliary/scanner/ftp/anonymous</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span> <span class="val">192.168.1.10</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>

<span class="cmt"># SSH Version Detection</span>
<span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">auxiliary/scanner/ssh/ssh_version</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span> <span class="val">192.168.1.0/24</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">THREADS</span> <span class="val">10</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠️ Caution</div>
      <p>Port scanners generate network noise. On monitored networks, they will likely trigger IDS/IPS alerts. Always scan at low thread counts in real engagements unless speed is the priority and stealth is not.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

