  <!-- SECTION 10 -->
  <section class="section" id="s10">
    <h2>10. Ethics, Legality &amp; Lab Setup</h2>

    <h3>The Golden Rule of Ethical Hacking</h3>
    <div class="callout danger">
      <div class="callout-title">⚖️ Always Get Written Permission</div>
      <p>Never run Metasploit against any system you do not own or have explicit written authorization to test. Unauthorized computer access is a serious federal crime (Computer Fraud and Abuse Act in the USA; similar laws globally). The consequences include heavy fines and imprisonment.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Legal Practice Environments</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Your own lab</strong> — Metasploitable 2/3, DVWA, VulnHub machines in VirtualBox/VMware</li>
      <li><strong>TryHackMe</strong> (tryhackme.com) — Guided rooms with legal targets; beginner-friendly</li>
      <li><strong>HackTheBox</strong> (hackthebox.com) — Real machines with CTF-style flags; intermediate/advanced</li>
      <li><strong>OffSec Proving Grounds</strong> — Practice machines from the OSCP creators</li>
      <li><strong>Bug Bounty Programs</strong> — Companies like HackerOne and Bugcrowd connect you with authorized targets</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Certifications to Pursue</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Certification</th><th>Level</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>CompTIA Security+</td><td>Beginner</td><td>Security fundamentals, compliance</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CEH (EC-Council)</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Ethical hacking methodology</td></tr>
          <tr><td>eJPT (eLearnSecurity)</td><td>Beginner</td><td>Practical pentesting; Metasploit focus</td></tr>
          <tr><td>OSCP (Offensive Security)</td><td>Advanced</td><td>Manual exploitation; 24-hour exam; gold standard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PNPT (TCM Security)</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Practical Network Penetration Testing</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Responsible Disclosure</h3>
    <p>If you discover a real, previously unknown vulnerability during authorized testing or personal research, the ethical path is <strong>responsible disclosure</strong>: notify the vendor privately, give them time (typically 90 days) to patch it, then publicly disclose to the community so others can protect themselves. This is how the security ecosystem improves over time.</p>

    <div class="callout success">
      <div class="callout-title">🏆 Key Takeaway</div>
      <p>The goal of learning Metasploit is not to break things. It is to understand how breaking happens so that we can build systems that don't break. Security knowledge is most valuable when used to <strong>defend, protect, and educate</strong>.</p>
    </div>

